%[text] # comp.ClosedBox Class
%[text] **J.G. Vermond, JVsound**
%[text] Closed-box enclosure: the rear of the diaphragm works on a sealed volume, the front radiates.
%[text] **Namespace:** comp
%[text] **Superclasses:** [comp.Enclosure](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','enclosure','enclosuredoc.m')))
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] The `comp.ClosedBox` class models a closed-box enclosure. The rear of the diaphragm of the driver in `Driver` works on the air in a sealed volume `RearVolume`; the front of the diaphragm radiates into a half space, as a rigid piston in an infinite baffle. `lspsys` uses the enclosure in the acoustical side of its network, see [solve2PortNetwork](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','methods','solve2portnetworkdoc','solve2portnetworkdoc.m'))). The symbols are listed in [Symbols](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','symbols.m'))). A worked example of a closed-box loudspeaker is [closedBoxExample](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'examples','closedBoxExample.m'))).
%%
%[text] ### Acoustical model
%[text] The acoustical side of the system is the two-port network $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $, which holds the 4-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $ of the enclosure and the 4-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $ of the radiation on three conductors: the front of the diaphragm (top), the ambient pressure (middle, the reference) and the rear of the diaphragm (bottom). For a closed box:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:c1b0)
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $: the air behind the diaphragm that moves with it is the acoustic mass $ M\_a = \\frac{M\_{mi}}{S\_d^2} $, in series in the rear conductor, because the full volume velocity $ U\_d $ flows through it. The air in the box is the acoustic compliance $ C\_a = \\frac{V\_r}{\\rho c^2} $, between the rear conductor and the ambient pressure. The front conductor passes through.
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $: the front of the diaphragm radiates through the radiation impedance $ Z\_{rad} $, between the front conductor and the ambient pressure. The radiation impedance is complex and is drawn as a box, as in Beranek. The rear does not radiate: the rear conductor passes through. \
%[text] The radiation impedance of a rigid piston with the radius $ r\_d $ in an infinite baffle (`zarad`), with the Bessel function $ J\_1 $ and the Struve function $ H\_1 $, is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ Z\_{rad} = \\frac{\\rho c}{\\pi r\_d^2} \\left( 1 - \\frac{J\_1(2 k r\_d)}{k r\_d} + j \\frac{H\_1(2 k r\_d)}{k r\_d} \\right) $
%[text] Its imaginary part is the air load on the front of the diaphragm. `comp.Driver` takes the air load on both sides out of the mechanical mass ($ M\_{md} = M\_{ms} - 2 M\_{mi} $), so $ Z\_{rad} $ and $ M\_a $ put it back at the front and at the rear. The mass is in series; the compliance and the radiation impedance are shunts to the ambient pressure. With the flows to the right on the front conductor and to the left on the rear conductor, the 4 × 4 transmission matrices are
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} = \\left\[ \\matrix{1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 1 - \\omega^2 M\_a C\_a & -j \\omega M\_a \\cr 0 & 0 & -j \\omega C\_a & 1} \\right\], \\quad \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} = \\left\[ \\matrix{1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\cr \\frac{1}{Z\_{rad}} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 0 & 1} \\right\] $
%[text] $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $ is the product of the series mass and the shunt compliance; the minus signs follow from the direction of the rear flows. With the method of `solve2PortNetwork`, these matrices give the two-port network $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $. With port 2 open, the diaphragm works on the acoustic impedance
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ Z\_a = \\frac{T\_{a,11}}{T\_{a,21}} = Z\_{rad} + j \\omega M\_a + \\frac{1}{j \\omega C\_a} $
%[text] so that $ p\_1 = Z\_a U\_d $: the radiation impedance, the air mass and the compliance of the box in series, as in the closed-box circuit of Beranek. All volume velocity of the diaphragm flows through $ Z\_{rad} $, so the radiated volume velocity is $ U\_{rad} = U\_d $.
%%
%[text] ## Creation
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] obj = comp.ClosedBox
%[text] ```
%[text] Creates a closed box with a rear volume of 10 L and a default driver, `comp.Driver`, which has all its parameters set to zero. Set the parameters of the driver before you use the enclosure.
%%
%[text] ## Properties
%[text] The properties are listed in the order of the class definition files.
%%
%[text] ### `RearVolume` — Volume of the rear chamber
%[text] `(1,1) double` \[m3\]
%[text] Volume $ V\_r $ of the sealed chamber behind the diaphragm; the literature (Small, Beranek) writes it as $ V\_b $. Must be positive. Default: 10e-3 m3 (10 L).
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `Driver` — Driver in the enclosure
%[text] `(1,1) comp.Driver`
%[text] Driver that is mounted in the enclosure. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. Default: `comp.Driver`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ## Methods
%[text:table]
%[text] | Method | Type | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `tae` | Instance | 4 × 4 transmission matrix $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $ of the enclosure, with the air mass $ M\_a $ in series and the compliance $ C\_a $ of the closed volume as a shunt on the rear conductor. |
%[text] | `tarad` | Instance | 4 × 4 transmission matrix $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $ of the radiation, with the radiation impedance $ Z\_{rad} $ as a shunt on the front conductor. |
%[text] | `zaFront` | Instance | Load $ Z\_{a,f} $ on the front of the diaphragm; for a closed box $ Z\_{rad} $. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. |
%[text] | `zaRear` | Instance | Load $ Z\_{a,r} $ on the rear of the diaphragm; for a closed box $ j \\omega M\_a + \\frac{1}{j \\omega C\_a} $. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. |
%[text] | `micTransfer` | Instance | Transfer to the microphone; empty for a closed box, so that `result` calculates the pressure from the radiated volume velocity. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. |
%[text] | `zarad` | Static | Radiation impedance of a rigid circular piston in an infinite baffle. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. |
%[text] | `struve` | Static | Approximation of the Struve function $ H\_1 $. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Examples
%%
%[text] ### Example: Acoustic impedance of the closed box
%[text] Create a driver according to the specifications of a B&C 21SW152-8, mount it in a closed box of 200 liter, and plot the magnitude of the acoustic impedance $ Z\_a = Z\_{rad} + j \\omega M\_a + \\frac{1}{j \\omega C\_a} $ that the diaphragm works on, from its three elements.
d = comp.Driver;
d.Re = 6;
d.Le = 2.2e-3;
d.Qes = 0.38;
d.Qms = 6.4;
d.Fs = 32;
d.Sd = 1680e-4;
d.Vas = 200e-3;
e = comp.ClosedBox;
e.RearVolume = 200e-3;
e.Driver = d;
f = logspace(log10(20),log10(20e3),1000);
w = 2*pi*f;
Ma = d.Mmi/d.Sd^2;
Ca = e.RearVolume/(lspsys.AirDensity*lspsys.SpeedOfSound^2);
Za = comp.Enclosure.zarad(d.DiaphragmRadius,w) + 1i*w*Ma + 1./(1i*w*Ca);
loglog(f,abs(Za),LineWidth=1.5) %[output:98ad4870]
grid on %[output:98ad4870]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:98ad4870]
ylabel("Acoustic impedance [Pa s/m^3]") %[output:98ad4870]
title("Acoustic impedance of the closed box") %[output:98ad4870]

